Events.RelatedBody:
**Describe the bug**

Open an ISO Strict package for editing and delete a part, for example the `CalculationChainPart` or a `WorksheetPart`. When the package is saved, the SDK puts the relationship to the deleted part back. The part itself is gone, and so is its content-type override. The saved package now has a dangling relationship. When you open it again with the SDK and read any part, you get:

```
System.InvalidOperationException:  Part: /xl/calcChain.xml doesn't exist in the package.

Original Message:Specified part does not exist in the package.
   at DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging.OpenXmlPart.Load(OpenXmlPackage openXmlPackage, OpenXmlPart parent, Uri uriTarget, String id)
   at DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging.PartRelationshipsFeature.LoadReferencedPartsAndRelationships()
   at DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging.PartRelationshipsFeature.get_RelationshipMap()
   at DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging.PartRelationshipsFeature.DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Features.IPartRelationshipsFeature.get_Count()
   at DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging.OpenXmlPackage.LoadAllParts()
   at DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Features.StrictNamespaceExtensions.StrictNamespaceFeature.DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Features.IStrictNamespaceFeature.get_Found()
   at DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging.OpenXmlPackage.get_StrictRelationshipFound()
   at DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging.OpenXmlPart.LoadDomTree[T]()
   at DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging.WorkbookPart.get_Workbook()
```

`SpreadsheetDocument.Open` itself succeeds. The exception comes on the first read of a part's root element.

Transitional packages are not affected. Version 2.20.0 is not affected. Versions 3.0.0, 3.4.1 and 3.5.1 are (the versions tested).

**Screenshots**

Not applicable.

**To Reproduce**

A self-contained console app. It needs only `DocumentFormat.OpenXml`. It builds a minimal Strict workbook in memory with `System.IO.Packaging`, so no test file is needed.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.IO.Packaging;
using System.Text;
using DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Packaging;

const string Rel = "http://purl.oclc.org/ooxml/officeDocument/relationships";
const string Main = "http://purl.oclc.org/ooxml/spreadsheetml/main";

using var stream = new MemoryStream();

// 1. Write a minimal Strict workbook: one sheet and a calcChain.
using (var package = Package.Open(stream, FileMode.Create, FileAccess.ReadWrite))
{
    var workbook = AddPart(package, "/xl/workbook.xml", "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet.main+xml",
        $"<workbook xmlns=\"{Main}\" xmlns:r=\"{Rel}\" conformance=\"strict\"><sheets><sheet name=\"Sheet1\" sheetId=\"1\" r:id=\"rId1\"/></sheets></workbook>");
    AddPart(package, "/xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml", "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.worksheet+xml",
        $"<worksheet xmlns=\"{Main}\"><sheetData/></worksheet>");
    AddPart(package, "/xl/calcChain.xml", "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.calcChain+xml",
        $"<calcChain xmlns=\"{Main}\"><c r=\"A1\" i=\"1\"/></calcChain>");

    package.CreateRelationship(workbook.Uri, TargetMode.Internal, Rel + "/officeDocument", "rId1");
    workbook.CreateRelationship(new Uri("worksheets/sheet1.xml", UriKind.Relative), TargetMode.Internal, Rel + "/worksheet", "rId1");
    workbook.CreateRelationship(new Uri("calcChain.xml", UriKind.Relative), TargetMode.Internal, Rel + "/calcChain", "rId2");
}

// 2. Open it for editing, delete the calcChain part, save.
using (var doc = SpreadsheetDocument.Open(stream, true))
{
    var workbookPart = doc.WorkbookPart!;
    workbookPart.DeletePart(workbookPart.CalculationChainPart!);
}

// 3. The saved workbook part still has a relationship to /xl/calcChain.xml.
using (var package = Package.Open(stream, FileMode.Open, FileAccess.Read))
{
    foreach (var r in package.GetPart(new Uri("/xl/workbook.xml", UriKind.Relative)).GetRelationships())
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{r.Id} {r.RelationshipType} -> {r.TargetUri}");
    }

    Console.WriteLine($"/xl/calcChain.xml exists: {package.PartExists(new Uri("/xl/calcChain.xml", UriKind.Relative))}");
}

// 4. Opening the saved package again fails.
using (var doc = SpreadsheetDocument.Open(stream, false))
{
    _ = doc.WorkbookPart!.Workbook; // InvalidOperationException: Part: /xl/calcChain.xml doesn't exist in the package.
}

Console.WriteLine("No exception.");

static PackagePart AddPart(Package package, string uri, string contentType, string xml)
{
    var part = package.CreatePart(new Uri(uri, UriKind.Relative), contentType);
    using var s = part.GetStream(FileMode.Create, FileAccess.Write);
    s.Write(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(xml));
    return part;
}
```

Output with 3.5.1:

```
rId1 http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/worksheet -> worksheets/sheet1.xml
rId2 http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/calcChain -> calcChain.xml
/xl/calcChain.xml exists: False
Unhandled exception. System.InvalidOperationException:  Part: /xl/calcChain.xml doesn't exist in the package.
```

The same thing happens:

- when the package is opened from a file path instead of a stream;
- when you delete a `WorksheetPart` instead of the calc chain (`Part: /xl/worksheets/sheet2.xml doesn't exist in the package.`);
- with a real Strict workbook saved by Excel 2013, and with `O14ISOStrict/Excel/2D Rotation-O12-XL-OartEffects.xlsx` from this repo's test assets.

If you change the two namespace constants to their transitional values, the repro prints `No exception.`

**Observed behavior**

After a part is deleted from a Strict package, the save writes a relationship to that part. The relationship has the transitional type. The SDK cannot read the saved package again.

**Expected behavior**

The deleted part's relationship is not in the saved package, as with a transitional package. The saved package opens again without error.

**Desktop (please complete the following information):**

- OS: Windows 11
- Office version: not needed to reproduce
- .NET Target: .NET 10 (net10.0)
- DocumentFormat.OpenXml Version: 3.0.0, 3.4.1 and 3.5.1 fail. 2.20.0 works. Also reproduced on `main` at 431ab05c.

**Additional context**

*Root cause*

[`StrictNamespaceExtensions.UseTransitionalRelationshipNamespaces`](https://github.com/dotnet/Open-XML-SDK/blob/431ab05cf160248cc3885a4a766026d4f8243792/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Features/StrictNamespaceExtensions.cs#L14-L80) does two things:

1. A [relationship filter](https://github.com/dotnet/Open-XML-SDK/blob/431ab05cf160248cc3885a4a766026d4f8243792/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Features/StrictNamespaceExtensions.cs#L24-L41) changes each Strict relationship type to its transitional type when the relationship is loaded. It records each changed relationship in `alteredRelationships`, by the URI of the part that owns it.
2. A [save callback](https://github.com/dotnet/Open-XML-SDK/blob/431ab05cf160248cc3885a4a766026d4f8243792/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Features/StrictNamespaceExtensions.cs#L45-L78) then calls `Relationships.Remove(id)` and `Relationships.Create(target, mode, type, id)` for every recorded relationship ([L58-L59](https://github.com/dotnet/Open-XML-SDK/blob/431ab05cf160248cc3885a4a766026d4f8243792/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Features/StrictNamespaceExtensions.cs#L58-L59), [L71-L72](https://github.com/dotnet/Open-XML-SDK/blob/431ab05cf160248cc3885a4a766026d4f8243792/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Features/StrictNamespaceExtensions.cs#L71-L72)).

The callback does not check that the relationship is still there. `DeletePart` removes the relationship ([OpenXmlPartContainer.cs L1458](https://github.com/dotnet/Open-XML-SDK/blob/431ab05cf160248cc3885a4a766026d4f8243792/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Packaging/OpenXmlPartContainer.cs#L1458), [L1466](https://github.com/dotnet/Open-XML-SDK/blob/431ab05cf160248cc3885a4a766026d4f8243792/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Packaging/OpenXmlPartContainer.cs#L1466)), but the record stays. On save, `Create` adds the relationship again. `System.IO.Packaging` does not check that the target exists. Nothing later in the save removes it.

The same code causes a second problem. Delete a part and then add a new part with the same relationship id (for example, `AddNewPart<WorksheetPart>("rId2")` after deleting the calc chain at `rId2`). The save replaces the new relationship with the old one. The new part loses its relationship, and the id points to the deleted part again.

*A related ordering problem*

The callback also returns early when `alteredRelationships` is empty ([L47-L50](https://github.com/dotnet/Open-XML-SDK/blob/431ab05cf160248cc3885a4a766026d4f8243792/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Features/StrictNamespaceExtensions.cs#L47-L50)). `SavePartContents` calls it for the package first ([OpenXmlPackage.cs L348](https://github.com/dotnet/Open-XML-SDK/blob/431ab05cf160248cc3885a4a766026d4f8243792/src/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Framework/Packaging/OpenXmlPackage.cs#L348)), then for each part. Relationships load lazily, so a relationship can be recorded after its owner's callback has run. If you open a Strict package and close it without reading anything, the part XML is written with transitional namespaces, but `_rels/.rels` (and, for the minimal workbook above, `xl/_rels/workbook.xml.rels`) keep their Strict types. Reading `StrictRelationshipFound` first loads all parts and avoids this.

*Fix*

PR #2156 fixes this. In short:

- A recorded relationship is rewritten only if its collection still holds that same relationship object. A check on `Contains(id)` alone fixes the deleted-part case but not the reused-id case. The reference check covers both.
- A relationship hidden by an ignore filter (for example after `IgnoreCalculationChainPartRelationship()`) is never in the collection, and cannot be removed through it. It is rewritten as before, so it is still written with its transitional type.
- The save callback loads every part before it rewrites anything, whichever container it is called for first. This fixes the ordering problem. It adds no real cost: `TrySavePartContent` already reads `StrictRelationshipFound` for every part, and that loads all parts.

The PR adds six tests in `test/DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Tests/IsoStrictTest/StrictRelationshipSaveTests.cs`. All six fail on `main` and pass with the fix:

- a deleted worksheet in the existing `2D Rotation-O12-XL-OartEffects.xlsx` asset;
- a deleted calc chain;
- a deleted package-level part (extended properties);
- a reused relationship id;
- an ignored relationship is still written with its transitional type;
- an open and close with nothing read writes every relationship with its transitional type.

The existing suites pass on net10.0: DocumentFormat.OpenXml.Tests (2,319 passed, 3 skipped as before), Packaging.Tests (190), Framework.Tests (192), Framework.Features.Tests (7) and Linq.Tests (22).

*Workaround*

Before you edit, open the Strict package, read `StrictRelationshipFound`, and close it. Then open it again and make the edits. The first close writes every relationship with its transitional type while all targets still exist. The second session then has no Strict relationships to restore. Reading `StrictRelationshipFound` matters. A plain open and close, or a `Save()` call before the edit, does not prevent the bug, because of the ordering problem above. Reading `StrictRelationshipFound` and then calling `Save()` in the same session also works.

*Possibly the same bug*

#1729 reports the same exception when the code reads any part after it deleted parts, on 3.x but not on 2.20. The list of deleted parts there includes `CalculationChainPart`. The maintainers could not reproduce it with the attached file. The reporter's tool converts workbooks to Strict conformance before it removes parts, so the Strict path may be the missing step.
